## Exiting Loops Early with `break`

`break` says "I'm done here" — it immediately exits the loop, no matter what the condition says.

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var i = 0;
  while i < 100 {
    io.println("Searching...");
    if i == 5 {
      io.println("Found it!");
      break;  // Exit the loop now — don't check i < 100 again
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

### The Search Pattern

This is the most common use of `break`:

```xiom
var found = false;
var i = 0;
while i < 1000 {
  if some_condition(i) {
    found = true;
    break;  // Found what we're looking for — stop!
  }
  i = i + 1;
}
```

### Break vs Letting the Loop End

```xiom
// Without break — runs all 100 iterations
var i = 0;
while i < 100 {
  io.println("Processing...");
  i = i + 1;
}

// With break — stops at iteration 10
var j = 0;
while j < 100 {
  io.println("Processing...");
  if j == 9 {
    break;  // Enough!
  }
  j = j + 1;
}
```

### Break in Infinite Loops

`break` is essential for infinite loops:

```xiom
while true {
  io.println("Menu:");
  io.println("1. Continue");
  io.println("2. Quit");
  // In a real program, you'd read user input here
  break;  // For now, just exit
}
```

### Break Only Exits One Loop

If you have nested loops, `break` only exits the INNERMOST loop:

```xiom
var i = 0;
while i < 3 {
  var j = 0;
  while j < 3 {
    if j == 1 {
      break;  // Exits inner loop only — outer loop continues!
    }
    j = j + 1;
  }
  i = i + 1;
}
```

